Kaelen worked differently than others. He shaped clay vessels not just to hold water or grain, but to capture light itself. Each dawn, he would set his pots outside to drink in the sunrise, sealing them before dusk. His neighbors thought him mad.

One moonless night, when the darkness felt especially heavy, Kaelen climbed to the village square carrying all his vessels—hundreds of them, accumulated over years. The people gathered, curious despite their fear.

He began breaking the pots, one by one. As each shattered, the trapped sunlight burst forth, not falling to earth but rising upward, seeking the sky. Golden fragments spiraled higher and higher, each one sustained by the hope Kaelen had poured into its making.

The lights didn't fade. They settled into the vast darkness above, burning steadily—some bright, some dim, but all enduring.

